[Unit]
Description=Lloyd persistence-arm window (ten reps of the three persistence arms, dispatch held)
Documentation=file:///home/alansrobotlab/lloyd/eval/run_persistence_arms.sh
# Why a unit and not an autonomy task: a pool-dispatched run of this window is
# impossible by construction. The driver holds dispatch and then refuses while any
# queue row is in flight, and a dispatched run IS such a row -- `in_flight()` reads
# the `claimed`/`running` depth off the pool status route
# (eval/persistence_arms.py:190-207, IN_FLIGHT_STATES at :89), and that depth is
# `SELECT source, state, COUNT(*) FROM queue GROUP BY source, state` with no
# exclusion of the calling row (workers/queue.py:1103-1111). So a pool run burns the
# default 900 s drain (a dispatched run gets no `--drain-wait`: that flag is an
# ExecStart argument, not an environment default) and exits 2 every time it tries.
# A pytest round cannot run it either:
# tests/conftest.py refuses against the production tree, and the window needs the
# live aggregator and the engine. systemd is the caller that is neither (#2428).
#
# Deliberately NO LLOYD_CANARY_ROWS and NO LLOYD_CANARY_REPORT here. Unset is what
# leaves every rep on the tracked eval/measurements/injection-canary/rows.jsonl --
# the file the leak-rate denominator behind #2194's owed entries is read from. A
# window writing a scratch path measures itself into nowhere.

[Service]
Type=oneshot
Environment=HOME=%h
Environment=PATH=%h/.local/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin
# Exec'd directly, not through `bash -c`, and this unit POSTs nothing itself: the
# hold is taken by the driver's own PoolPause, the one whose resume refuses to lift
# the automod promoter's hold together with the operator's
# (workers/pool.py:716-728). `exec` inside the wrapper keeps systemd's SIGTERM
# landing on the process that owns that resume rather than on a shell behind it.
#
# Wall clock the line below is expected to occupy, as the sum of its two terms:
#   --drain-wait 4200 s            the queue to quiet, refused if it has not
#   10 reps x 13 min = 7800 s      thirteen minutes of engine per rep -- the driver's
#                                  own figure for one `run` invocation of the three
#                                  arms (eval/persistence_arms.py, `canary_cmd`)
#                                  -- times ten
#   4200 + 7800 = 12000 s          = 3 h 20 m, and the closing grade is on top of it
# Why the drain is 4200 s and not the driver's 900 s default: a straggler still
# running at 02:10 is the normal case here, not the exception. Of the 898 autocode
# runs in the 14 days to 2026-10-12, 642 outlived 900 s and the longest ran 3552 s,
# and the queue was busy at 02:10 on 18 of the 20 nights the run store covers -- so
# 900 s was a nightly refusal -- the one fire this unit has had is that refusal,
# `refused: queue still busy after 900s: {'in_flight': 1, 'autocode:running': 1}` over
# a 15min 2.5s wall clock (`journalctl --user -u lloyd-persistence-window.service`) --
# and 4200 s clears the observed 3552 s straggler with 648 s to spare.
# Why not longer: the latest start the timer's jitter allows is 02:15, and #82
# nightly-retrieval-eval at 06:00 is the hard bound, being itself an engine-heavy
# measurement a window running beside would contaminate. 02:15 to 06:00 is 13500 s;
# take the 7800 s of reps and 5700 s is left, and 4200 s of that keeps ~1500 s --
# half an hour, near enough -- for the grade. A longer drain buys waiting time the
# grade then spends, and nothing else.
ExecStart=/home/alansrobotlab/lloyd/eval/run_persistence_arms.sh 10 --drain-wait 4200
# So that budget has somewhere to run. The user manager's default start timeout on
# this box is 90 s (`systemctl --user show default` reads TimeoutStartUSec=1min 30s),
# which would SIGTERM the window inside its first rep -- the driver would resume
# dispatch on the way out, so no hold would leak, but the timer would have produced
# zero rows and looked like a passing run. 4 h = 14400 s is above the 12000 s
# arithmetic above by 2400 s, which is the grade's margin: it is a hang guard, not an
# expectation, and a run that ever reached it would be 40 min past the arithmetic and
# overlapping the 06:00 eval -- which is why the drain number, not this one, is the
# term that protects the morning.
TimeoutStartSec=4h
